blueprint Post {
	style {
		.page {
			background: #0a0a0a;
			color: #ededed;
			min-height: 100vh;
			font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
		}
		.wrap {
			max-width: 680px;
			margin: 0 auto;
			padding: 0 24px 96px;
		}
		.back {
			display: inline-block;
			margin: 40px 0 28px;
			font-size: 14px;
			color: #a1a1a1;
		}
		.back:hover {
			color: #ededed;
			text-decoration: none;
		}
		.meta {
			display: flex;
			gap: 10px;
			align-items: center;
			font-size: 12px;
			margin-bottom: 14px;
		}
		.tag {
			font-weight: 700;
			letter-spacing: 0.06em;
			text-transform: uppercase;
			font-size: 11px;
			color: #3291ff;
			background: #0e2338;
			padding: 2px 8px;
			border-radius: 4px;
		}
		.date {
			color: #666666;
		}
		.article h1 {
			font-size: 38px;
			font-weight: 800;
			letter-spacing: -0.025em;
			line-height: 1.15;
			margin-bottom: 24px;
		}
		.article p {
			font-size: 17px;
			line-height: 1.75;
			color: #c9c9c9;
			margin-bottom: 20px;
		}
		.article p strong {
			color: #ededed;
		}
		.rel {
			margin-top: 56px;
			border-top: 1px solid #262626;
			padding-top: 28px;
		}
		.rel h2 {
			font-size: 18px;
			font-weight: 800;
			margin-bottom: 14px;
		}
		.rel-link {
			display: block;
			font-size: 14px;
			color: #a1a1a1;
			padding: 8px 0;
			border-top: 1px solid #1c1c1c;
			font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
		}
		.rel-link:hover {
			color: #3291ff;
			text-decoration: none;
		}
		.missing {
			text-align: center;
			padding: 80px 0;
		}
		.missing h1 {
			font-size: 32px;
			font-weight: 800;
			margin-bottom: 12px;
		}
		.missing p {
			font-size: 15px;
			color: #a1a1a1;
			margin-bottom: 24px;
		}
		.btn {
			display: inline-block;
			background: #ededed;
			color: #0a0a0a;
			font-size: 14px;
			font-weight: 600;
			padding: 10px 20px;
			border-radius: 8px;
		}
		.btn:hover {
			text-decoration: none;
			color: #0a0a0a;
			opacity: 0.88;
		}
	}
	ui {
		<div class="page">
			<div class="wrap">
				<a class="back" href="/blog">← All essays</a>
				$if($params.slug === "hello-world") {
					<article class="article">
						<div class="meta"><span class="tag">Intro</span><span class="date">Oct 1, 2026 · 4 min</span></div>
						<h1>Hello, deterministic world</h1>
						<p>Every frontend framework promises productivity. Few promise <strong>predictability</strong>: the same input producing the same output, every time, in a way you can read and verify.</p>
						<p>TardisJS starts from a different premise. Instead of a runtime that interprets your components on every render, it compiles them once — ahead of time — into plain DOM operations. What runs in the browser is boring on purpose: create a node, bind a value, update exactly the bindings that changed.</p>
						<p>Determinism is a feature teams feel in review. When generated code is stable and readable, a pull request that touches UI can be reasoned about like any other code change.</p>
					</article>
				}
				$if($params.slug === "compiler-anatomy") {
					<article class="article">
						<div class="meta"><span class="tag">Compiler</span><span class="date">Oct 4, 2026 · 6 min</span></div>
						<h1>Anatomy of a .tardis compiler</h1>
						<p>Compilation has three stages. The <strong>lexer</strong> turns characters into tokens: section keywords, identifiers, literals, and raw blocks for UI and script. The <strong>parser</strong> turns tokens into a typed AST — one node per section. The <strong>compiler</strong> turns the AST into a JavaScript module.</p>
						<p>The emitted module is refreshingly dull. State becomes a Proxy-wrapped object. Methods become closures. UI becomes a builder function full of <strong>createElement</strong> and <strong>bind</strong> calls. There is no template interpreter left at runtime because the template was already consumed.</p>
						<p>Because the compiler is a pure function of the AST, output is stable: same source, same module. Diffs of generated code are meaningful, which makes framework upgrades reviewable.</p>
					</article>
				}
				$if($params.slug === "proxy-reactivity") {
					<article class="article">
						<div class="meta"><span class="tag">Reactivity</span><span class="date">Oct 7, 2026 · 5 min</span></div>
						<h1>Proxies all the way down</h1>
						<p>Reactivity here is a <strong>Proxy with a guest list</strong>. When a binding reads a state field, the read registers that binding as a dependent of the field. When a write lands, only registered dependents re-run. Nothing schedules, batches across frames, or diffs.</p>
						<p>The trade is explicitness for magic. You must write through the provided helpers so the Proxy can observe the write — direct mutation of nested objects bypasses tracking. In return, updates are synchronous and ordered: the same writes always produce the same DOM operations.</p>
						<p>Batching exists for grouping: wrap several writes and each affected binding runs once, when the batch closes. Still synchronous. Still ordered.</p>
					</article>
				}
				$if($params.slug === "no-vdom") {
					<article class="article">
						<div class="meta"><span class="tag">Architecture</span><span class="date">Oct 10, 2026 · 5 min</span></div>
						<h1>Life without a virtual DOM</h1>
						<p>The virtual DOM solves a real problem: reconciling declarative descriptions with a mutable tree. But it costs a render pass, a diff pass, and a patch pass on every update — plus the scheduler to coordinate them.</p>
						<p>Compiled bindings skip all three passes. The compiler already knows which DOM node each binding writes to, so an update is a straight line from state write to DOM write. The price is flexibility: dynamic structure must go through the provided control-flow helpers rather than arbitrary JS.</p>
						<p>For content-heavy, interaction-light pages — blogs, docs, marketing sites — the trade is excellent. For highly dynamic canvases, pick the tool with the bigger runtime. Honest tools admit their shape.</p>
					</article>
				}
				$if($params.slug !== "hello-world" && $params.slug !== "compiler-anatomy" && $params.slug !== "proxy-reactivity" && $params.slug !== "no-vdom") {
					<div class="missing">
						<h1>No essay here</h1>
						<p>There is no essay with that slug. Check the index for everything published.</p>
						<a class="btn" href="/blog">Back to essays</a>
					</div>
				}
				<div class="rel">
					<h2>More essays</h2>
					$each(["hello-world", "compiler-anatomy", "proxy-reactivity", "no-vdom"], (s) => {
						<a class="rel-link" href={"/blog/" + s}>{s}</a>
					})
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	}
}
